or not these fears were based in  reality is not entirely certain but  absinthe was banned in the United States  in 1915 regardless as well as in much of  Europe including France the Netherlands  Belgium Switzerland and austria-hungary  in more recent years studies have shown  that absent psychoactive properties are  greatly exaggerated and the Green Fairy  is back in production in over a dozen  countries and back on the shelves of  stores around the world so now if you  want to you can try the legendary spirit  for yourself just be warned that while  it might not be hallucinogenic it's  still a pretty potent alcohol usually  between 45 and 74 percent ABV next up we  have this filthy concoction courtesy of  the good old USA  Four Loko was released in the US market  in 2005 and is currently available in 49  states and in 21 countries around the  world the four loko you can buy today is  a much tamer beast than the original  which was banned in several states after  legal ethical and health concerns were  raised regarding the marketing practices  and contents of the beverage alcohol  alone can be dangerous but when you  throw caffeine into the mix things can  get very bad very quickly the original  recipe called for the equivalent of four  standard drinks worth of alcohol five  cups of coffees worth of caffeine as  well as taurine and gharana popular  ingredients in energy drinks so when you  drank four loko in 2005 you were playing  Russian roulette by combining large  quantities of uppers caffeine and  downers alcohol you could keep drinking  the stuff without feeling the onset of  the effects of alcohol so you'd keep  drinking more the fun part was guessing  whether you'd end up in an ambulance  from alcohol poisoning or a heart attack  luckily after the ban the company  changed the recipe and removed all  caffeine taurine and guarana and is no  longer marketed as an energy drink now  it's just a really inexpensive and  unpleasant way to consume a lot of  alcohol next up we have the Internet's  favorite challenge shot  the brutal grain alcohol known as  Everclear this american-made bottle of  liquid misery is available in two  strengths 151 and 190 proof or 75.5 and  95 percent alcohol by volume that's a  lot of alcohol so much that the sale of  the 190 proof is prohibited in some  states luckily I live in the lawless  wasteland that is Texas so what you're  seeing here is the big-boy version there  comes a point when your alcohol is just  too strong to drink for example some  alternative uses for Everclear include  the fact that it's often recommended as  a camping stove fuel substitute and that  carpenters often use it with shellac to  produce high-grade wood sealants on the  plus-side Everclear has a very neutral  flavor profile so if you want a really  strong drink that will play nicely with  your favorite mixers this would actually  mix pretty well just don't overdo it the  last drink on our list is about as  American as it gets  though originally brought over by  scots-irish immigrants when you hear the  word moonshine you probably think of a  bunch of Appalachian hillbillies hiding  from law enforcement out in the woods  and that's pretty much the gist of it in  the early 1900s moonshine provided a  huge boost in revenue for Appalachian  farmers since the limited roads made  difficult to transport corn crops they  quickly realized that a horse could  carry ten times the value of corn by  first converting it into whiskey while  the term moonshine originally referred  to illegally produced liquor it's since  been reclaimed by producers of legal  products in an effort to market them as  a similar drinking experience to the  original white lightning the problem  with early moonshine is that there was  no way to regulate it  if farmer Joe said his batch would get  you drunk you just have to take his word  for it and roll the dice you never knew  what was in the drink and impurities in  the still often caused health problems  including blindness and lead poisoning  proper moonshine finally became legal in  the United States in 2010 and is defined  as a clear on aged whiskey it's sale is  still heavily regulated by the ATF but  the stuff you buy at your local liquor  store is pretty much guaranteed not to  kill you or make you go blind at least  in reasonable quantities so if you're in  the mood for a drink once considered so  dangerous it had to be banned you've got  your short list just remember don't  drink if you're underage and for those  of you who do try these concoctions be  safe and drink responsibly and if you'd  rather play it safe and try some much  tastier drinks I highly recommend you  check out shaker and spoon shaker and  spoon is a monthly cocktail subscription  box that delivers a craft cocktail  experience right to your home every  month you'll get three brand-new  original recipes created by world-class  bartenders as well as enough ingredients  for 12 cocktails four from each recipe  each monthly box revolves around one  type of liqueur so you only need to add  the one bottle for all three recipes  don't worry if you're not a drink mixing  expert shaker and spoon provides easy to  follow instructions and how-to videos to  make your own beautiful complex  cocktails I'm certainly no professional  when it comes to mixing drinks but it's  a lot of fun to open the monthly box and  watch an awesome cocktail come together  as a little bonus for fans of second  thought anyone who signs up using the  link below will save $20 on their  subscription visit shaker and spoon comm  slash second thought to save on your  subscription and start making awesome  craft cocktails today if you enjoyed  this video make sure to subscribe and  click the little bell next to my channel  name to join the notification squad you  can watch my previous videos by clicking  here or watch my most popular videos by  clicking here thanks for watching and  I'll see you in the next video  you
 

